so collective commutation is how we  think biological systems solve problems  but before delving into what collective  commutation is it's worth stepping back  and thinking about the big picture  so in physical systems physics is  dominated by concepts like pressure  entropy and temperature and these arise  from simple collective interactions and  provide huge insight into how the  universe works in biological systems  there are similar collective concepts  like metabolism conflict management and  robustness but unlike in physical  systems these are functional concepts  and whereas in physical systems order is  generated through the minimization of  energy in biological systems there's the  additional step of information  processing why biological systems have  this additional step and whether  information processing makes them  fundamentally subjective and therefore  uncharacterized able by laws are big  open questions in C for my research  group here at SFI we think that  biological systems overcome this  intrinsic subjectivity by computing in  evolutionary developmental or learning  time their own macroscopic worlds in  other words consolidating regularities  to do work more efficiently examples of  collective computation include the  coherent behavior that arises at the  whole working as a level from the firing  decisions of the billions of neurons in  your brain it includes the features of  social structure like the distribution  of power that arrives from the status  signaling interactions in chimpanzee and  macaque groups and which have huge  implications for how conflict is managed  it includes the collective motion of  fish schools that arises from the  alignment and velocity decisions of  individual fish and it includes the gene  regulatory interactions in micaiah derms  that produce robust features of  phenotype
 

